How does qtl2 handle having many SNPs with the same genetic location? How does that change the association calculations?

On Friday, February 7, 2020 at 12:21:28 PM UTC-6, Karl Broman wrote:

> In calc_genoprob() attempt to reconstruct founder genotypes from the observed SNP data. This requires an understanding of the rate of recombination in each marker interval along the chromosome, which is where the genetic map is used. The physical map is not a suitable replacement.
>
> If you just want to plot the results relative to physical distance rather than relative to genetic distance, you could use
>
>    plot(allpheno$pmap, out)
>
> where you might have used
>
>   plot(allpheno$gmap, out)
>
> karl
>
> > On Feb 7, 2020, at 10:17 AM, Elsa de Becker <elsade...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Thanks both for the suggestions!
> >
> > I guess my real question is, what is the provided genetic map used for besides plotting LOD scores at the end? I ask because when I tried substituting the physical map for the genetic map as recommended above I got different LODs for each SNP, I assume because there are often >40 SNPs sharing a single genetic map position.
