MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I recently used r/qtl for mapping in a  population consisting of  BC2S3 lines from crosses of 10 diversity donors with a single reference parent.
I reduced the genotypes to reference and nonreference and followed the "Users Guide for New BCsFt Tools for R/qtl"  <https://rdrr.io/cran/qtl/f/inst/doc/bcsft.pdf>

I want to know if I can use r/qtl2 in this kind of population? Is this population a kind of ail?
Could I bring the genotype probabilities calculated in rqtl to rqtl2 and then use the linear mixed model mapping?

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Wed Feb 26 22:03:50 2020
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Wed, 26 Feb 2020 22:03:50 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl2-disc] Using r/qtl2 for a mulriple parent BCsFt population
Message-ID: <YUFXwuILJrM.k1WDcxd6CAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <YUFXwuILJrM.GT4o-Lp5CAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <YUFXwuILJrM.GT4o-Lp5CAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/YUFXwuILJrM/m/k1WDcxd6CAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I don’t think there is a cross type in qtl2 that would be appropriate, at present.
You can try converting the genotype probabilities using the probs_qtl_to_qtl2() function in the qtl2convert package, https://github.com/rqtl/qtl2convert

karl

